  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (through Saturday)...\n\nUpper low currently over Utah is expected to lift northeast into \nthe Dakotas by Monday afternoon. The track is a little further \neast and south than proggs from 24 hrs ago. This keeps the best \nchance of precip further east and closer to the Montana/Dakotas \nborder. Precipitation in the SE corner of MT should pick up by \nevening and nose northward into Fallon and possibly Custer \nCounties late tonight into Monday...and may become mixed \ndrizzle/rain/snow overnight as well with areas of fog. Road \nsurface temps may drop to the lower 30s so cannot rule out some \npatchy freezing drizzle either. Those with travel or commutes in \nsoutheast Montana Monday morning should plan for wet roads and \nreduced visibility at times. Most locations should see less than a\ntenth of an inch of liquid precip. Areas further west may see \nsome scattered to broken stratiform clouds late tonight and early \nMonday. \n\nA stronger upper trough is expected to aggressively push into the\nnorthern Rockies to start the work week, impacting our region \nlater Monday night through Tuesday evening. This system is progged\nto have a negative tilt as it rotates east/northeast across our \nregion. Rain/snow showers move into the mountains and western \nzones Monday night. Models suggest a strong downslope signature \nTuesday as the best upper dynamics and lift sweep across the rest \nof the CWA. So precip may limited in the immediate shadow of the \nmountains before picking up around Yellowstone county as flow \nbecomes removed from the downslope effects. Windy conditions \nshould be expected, with westerly gusts 35-45 mph common Tuesday \nfor many areas and a few gusts to near 50 mph possible. \n\nCurrently, the areas with the best chance of seeing at least \n0.25\" of precip is over the Beartooths (80%) and norther Rosebud \nCounty (57%). Over the mountains, snow is favored over west \naspects of the Beartooth/Absaroka and Crazy mountains, with a \n65-80% chance for at least 3 inches above 7500 ft. The foothills, \nsuch at Red Lodge may not see much if any snow accumulation, \nthough a mix of snow and rain is possible Tuesday morning. The \nchance of any significant snowfall over the northern Bighorn \nmountains has dropped, with just a 35% chance of around 1\" \naccumulation on west slopes. \n\nFor the last half of the work week, the pattern will transition \nto southwest flow aloft over our region. This may bring in some\noccasional isolated rain/snow showers to our western mountains and\nnearby areas, but the lower elevations will likely remain mostly\ndry and just breezy at times. Look for temps to remain around or \njust under season averages. BT",
